From: "Michael Sprong" <myk@qmail.idtinc.com>
Date: 5 May 1994 15:07:18 -0800
Subject: [#94-5-66] filtering

filtering
On Thu, 5 May 1994 R.Bryner@m.cc.utah.edu wrote in reply to
nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu:
>> Is there some elegant way to mark pieces that have significant
>> off-topic material?
>Yes, the first person reading this hits the vote key(on a hypothetical
> news/mail reader), selects "no informational content/off topic" from the
> menu, and hits return.  
We already make descisions on what we wish to keep after all chances a
t filtering have been made.
IE: When the user performs the actions needed to process the file.
 (delete, save, reply, forward, etc...)
In an adaptive filtering model, the editor software could communicate
 back to the filter the effective action taken by the user at the time of
 examination.
{
If the use deletes after examining the entire contents of the file, then an
 examination flag can be set in the filter's state machine.
If  the user deletes, outright, without examining the contents, then a kill
 flag can be set in the filter.
... there are many such criterion for the acceptance or rejection of a
 particular file.
}
Depending upon the available resources, these flags can be more than a simple
 flag which says delete this or that file due to a base context free
 assumption. In an adaptive model, the editor could inquire of the user some
 information concerning the file based upon a simple context-based grammer.
 Such an inquiry would only occur under some rather limited circumstances.
 If the editor needed more information to generally classify the file, or if
 the user explicity invoked the query. Otherwise the user would never see
 the basic model in operation, except to note that files they normally rejected
 immediately would no longer arrive at their desk. The user would be allowed
 to exend the basic set of acceptance/ rejection criterion using atoms of
 the origional grammer, thereby equiping the filter with selection information
 not normal to it's analysis method. The basic criterion themselves could be
 simple tags for undesirable words or phrases in the unquoted portion of the
 document, or the addresses of those whom you'd rather not hear from. Or
 as the editor learns from its user, more complex thing, such as text formatted
 for the description of the latest excerpts from the human genome project.
 Of course such a criterion tree could find itself collapsing if information is
 gathered for a long period of time, or without real regard for content
deleted.
 So a facility for optimising and combining rules would be needed.

Suppose, if every time you recieved a message from your friends in Florida,
 you read it and then replied to them. After a couple of times of doing this,
 the editor would ask you if your wished to reply after the entire message
 has been reviewed. Likewise, if some lunatic in the California State Prison
 system keeps sending you and your friends mass mailings of prison pity grams,
 your editor/filter would eventually eliminate then on the basis of send
address or
 even content.

If you don't like the idea of having a machine make important
 decisions for you, then the deletion of a file could be simply an archival
 process with a time limit on the persistance of a file, in addition to a
facility
 to retreave a formally deleted file.







