I agree that this is needed. To pick someone I know, I like reading Fnerd's
comments about politics, am not interested in his comments on Macintosh's,
and don't understand most of the discussion about PCR.  I'd like to read
all LANG and LINGUISTICS posts, PPL posts only if they deal with my PPL or
with general rules, and philosophy posts only if they are neither too
simple nor too technical for my level of interest -- I know 'em when I see
'em.

Seems like there are multiple axis. Author and topic are two. Degree of
"seriousness" is another. There's an axis between general theory, and
specific applications; another between today's applications and
post-Singularity (and post-collapse-of-this-particular-space-time-continuum.) 

One of the most important scales is the originality of the post: even if it
is a brilliant idea by a famous person, I don't want to read it over and
over again... but I sure want to read it at least once!

Here's an unmet need that I have: I am very interested in reproductive
freedom, an issue with two sides that are so polarized that very little
progress has been made towards any sort of political solution. I have
absolutely no interest in reading the same old arguments, even if I agree
with the authors. I would be very interested in seeing any new ideas. Any
group with a low signal-to-noise ratio could use a "stale idea" filter...










