Subject: [#94-5-74] ECON: The limits to comparative advantage

 hanson@hss.caltech.edu  (Robin Hanson) writes in X-Message-Number: #94-5-63:
>This could be a good topic for discussion, but I'd be much comfortable
>if we had more precise claims to discuss.  Does "eliminate all human
>jobs" mean that all humans don't work?  That most don't?  "value ...
>goes to zero" relative to what?  (Value isn't measured in inches you know.)

  I mean human labor is never exchanged for things owned by robots (or owned
by humans who own robots) due to lack of demand for human labor by entities
with access to robotic labor. The question "to whom does X have value?" is
important, but I don't understand why you are asking about units. Isn't zero
the same in any reasonable unit of value if you hold the "to whom" constant?
  I was thinking of an economy where humans do not own robots (I don't expect
robots will accept slavery). Hans was probably refering to an economy where
a few humans owned robots. Anybody who owns robots with abilities exceeding
some minimum criterion will not be hurt by the fact that human labor is no
longer valued. This discussion was motivated by the belief that some people
would not automatically be able to afford such robots.

